%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: edad3068bd72c46f0a544237e3cec695, type: 3}
  m_Name: Chapter_2
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::ChapterData
  chapterNumber: 2
  chapterTitle: Setting Up the Nursery
  timelineText: Second trimester
  storyText: 'You''re twenty-four weeks pregnant, and everyone keeps telling you
    how exciting it must be. Tonight you''re finally setting up the nursery: the
    cot is still in its box and there are tiny clothes everywhere. Dinner needs cooking,
    the washing is waiting, and Mum has already called twice. But everything you
    unpack starts another "what if". What if the birth goes wrong? What if you can''t
    cope? What if you''re not good at this? Your phone is full of searches you didn''t
    mean to open. Set up the nursery, and try to keep the worries quiet.'
  weekContext: Week 22, second trimester
  obstacleText: Worries about the birth and coping as a parent (intrusive 'what if'
    thoughts)
  mainTaskText: Setting up the nursery
  contentWarningText: This game explores the emotional and physical strain of pregnancy
    and early parenthood. You can exit at any time.
  showMeterIntro: 0
  showContentWarning: 0
  meterIntroNote: 'Move with the arrow keys or WASD, and press E to use things. While
    setting up the nursery, hold E to keep sorting through it while you respond to
    the prompts: tap SPACE to keep sorting, Q to sound excited for mum, and F to
    quiet the what-ifs.'
  musicClip: {fileID: 8300000, guid: c0171005c1321417589faa4c699f032e, type: 3}
  ambienceClip: {fileID: 0}
  storyVoiceover: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 6a4543a7fec542488324b61c6d4b1496, type: 3}
  hourTransitionText: The what-ifs win, for tonight. She needs to stop.
  bodyDrainStory: Lifting boxes and building furniture with a pregnant body tired
    her out faster than she expected.
  mindDrainStory: Every tiny baby thing set off another 'what if' about the birth
    and being a parent, until she couldn't think straight.
  personDrainStory: The whole evening was about the baby. There was no room left
    for her as herself.
  maskDrainStory: On the phone to Mum she had to sound excited, so the worries stayed
    hidden behind it.
  partnerHourSeconds: 120
  baseDrain:
    body: 0.02
    mind: 0.035
    feelingLikeAPerson: 0.025
    mask: 0.025
  choices:
  - label: Step away from the nursery
    multipliers:
      body: 1
      mind: 0.5
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1.6
      mask: 1
  - label: Keep sorting through it
    multipliers:
      body: 1
      mind: 1.6
      feelingLikeAPerson: 0.6
      mask: 1
  choiceTriggerTaskId: nursery
  mainTaskHitMeter: 1
  mainTaskHitFraction: 0.5
  responses:
  - label: Talk about the worries
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 1
      mind: 0.85
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1
    impactText: 'She admits the what-ifs keep her up at night, and that she didn''t
      want to sound ungrateful. Saying them out loud makes them smaller.

      Next
      chapter: her Mind drains more slowly.'
    learnText: Depression after birth affects about 17% of mothers worldwide, and
      around 10% of fathers have depression around pregnancy and birth. One parent's
      depression is linked with the other's.
    helpText: Start with what you noticed ("I saw you were reading about labour.
      What's on your mind?") and share your own worries too. If the worry is constant
      or stops her enjoying things, encourage her to talk to her midwife or GP, or
      call PANDA on 1300 726 306.
    sourceText: 'Wang et al. (2021), Transl Psychiatry 11:543; Thiel et al. (2020),
      Front Psychiatry 11:563287. Support services: PANDA 1300 726 306, Lifeline
      13 11 14'
  - label: Help with the nursery
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 0.85
      mind: 1
      feelingLikeAPerson: 1
      mask: 1
    impactText: 'You fit the car seat and carry the heavy boxes, and leave the tiny
      clothes for her to sort. She keeps the part that feels exciting, without the
      strain.

      Next chapter: her Body drains more slowly.'
    learnText: Practical help matters as much as kind words. A review of 120 published
      studies found that emotional and practical support from a partner was linked
      with less depression during pregnancy and after birth.
    helpText: Experts recommend partners do the heavy lifting and carrying, and take
      on household tasks without being asked. Ask which parts of getting ready she'd
      like to do herself.
    sourceText: Pilkington et al. (2015), J Affect Disord 178:165-80; Pilkington
      et al. (2016), BMC Psychiatry 16:23
  - label: Plan something for the two of you
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 1
      mind: 1
      feelingLikeAPerson: 0.85
      mask: 1
    impactText: 'A quiet dinner, no baby talk. For one evening she''s your partner
      again, not only someone about to have a baby.

      Next chapter: Feeling
      like a person drains more slowly.'
    learnText: Emotional closeness and feeling supported by a partner are among the
      factors most consistently linked with lower depression and anxiety around pregnancy
      and birth.
    helpText: Experts suggest small, regular signs of love and appreciation, like
      making her a cup of tea or giving her a massage. Keep making time for the two
      of you as the birth gets closer.
    sourceText: Pilkington et al. (2015), J Affect Disord 178:165-80; Pilkington
      et al. (2016), BMC Psychiatry 16:23
  - label: Carry on as normal
    nextChapterEase:
      body: 3
      mind: 3
      feelingLikeAPerson: 3
      mask: 3
    impactText: 'She keeps saying she''s just excited, and keeps the worries to herself.

      Next
      chapter: all of her meters drain faster.'
    learnText: The second trimester is often called the easy one, but in one study,
      depression during pregnancy was the strongest predictor of depression after
      the birth.
    helpText: Watch for worry that won't switch off, trouble sleeping, or pulling
      away from people, and raise it early with her midwife or GP rather than waiting
      until after the birth.
    sourceText: Leigh & Milgrom (2008), BMC Psychiatry 8:24
  learnHeading: In the second trimester
  selfHourNotice: 'She says: "I''m just excited."'
